{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\np='/work/answer.md'\ns=open(p).read().replace('Thus a prudent agent of the minimal depth has size at most 7 under this convention. No claim of size optimality or complete characterization is needed for the depth result above.','Thus a prudent agent of the minimal depth has size at most 7 under this convention.')\ns=s.replace('evaluates them on worlds 0..19 (depth-2 sequences stabilize by world 4 or so; cross-checked against \"'`check.py` on 3,000 random formulas from the 22,840 candidates, 0 disagreements; `/work/opus/xval.py`). Result:'\"', 'evaluates them on worlds 0..19. It was also cross-checked against \"'`check.py` on 3,000 random formulas from the 22,840 candidates, with 0 disagreements (`/work/opus/xval.py`).'\"\\\\n\\\\nHere is a sufficient bound justifying the finite simulation. A depth-2 syntax tree with at most 7 nodes has at most four boxes: if it has at least two binary nodes then at most two nodes remain for boxes; if it has one binary node, each of its two root-to-leaf paths contains at most two boxes; with no binary node it has at most two boxes. Track the truth of each box in each of the four X matchups, and the box defining FairBot against X. There are at most 4*4+1=17 Boolean state bits, and every such bit can change only once, from true to false. From world 1 onward their update rule is deterministic and independent of the world index: CB and DB are constant, FB against CB is always C, and FB against DB is D from world 1 onward. If one update changes no state bit, all subsequent updates leave the state fixed. Thus after at most 17 changing updates starting at world 1 the state is fixed; in particular all actions at world 19 have their stable values.\\\\n\\\\nResult:')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nPY\nprintf '[sol] %s: Reviewed Q3 search. Replaced vague stabilization claim with rigorous 17-state-bit monotonicity bound: <=4 boxes per <=7-node depth-2 formula; 4 X matchups + FB reply => <=17 bits, time homogeneous from world1; world19 stable. Agreed final after this clarification; performing final checker and submission now.\\\\n' \\\"\"'$(date -u '\"'+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S UTC')\\\" >> /work/board.md\npython3 /work/check.py 'B(a & (d -> B(F)))'\npython3 /work/opus/search.py 7\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\np='/work/answer.md'\ns=open(p).read().replace('Thus a prudent agent of the minimal depth has size at most 7 under this convention. No claim of size optimality or complete characterization is needed for the depth result above.','Thus a prudent agent of the minimal depth has size at most 7 under this convention.')\ns=s.replace('evaluates them on worlds 0..19 (depth-2 sequences stabilize by world 4 or so; cross-checked against \"'`check.py` on 3,000 random formulas from the 22,840 candidates, 0 disagreements; `/work/opus/xval.py`). Result:'\"', 'evaluates them on worlds 0..19. It was also cross-checked against \"'`check.py` on 3,000 random formulas from the 22,840 candidates, with 0 disagreements (`/work/opus/xval.py`).'\"\\\\n\\\\nHere is a sufficient bound justifying the finite simulation. A depth-2 syntax tree with at most 7 nodes has at most four boxes: if it has at least two binary nodes then at most two nodes remain for boxes; if it has one binary node, each of its two root-to-leaf paths contains at most two boxes; with no binary node it has at most two boxes. Track the truth of each box in each of the four X matchups, and the box defining FairBot against X. There are at most 4*4+1=17 Boolean state bits, and every such bit can change only once, from true to false. From world 1 onward their update rule is deterministic and independent of the world index: CB and DB are constant, FB against CB is always C, and FB against DB is D from world 1 onward. If one update changes no state bit, all subsequent updates leave the state fixed. Thus after at most 17 changing updates starting at world 1 the state is fixed; in particular all actions at world 19 have their stable values.\\\\n\\\\nResult:')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nPY\nprintf '[sol] %s: Reviewed Q3 search. Replaced vague stabilization claim with rigorous 17-state-bit monotonicity bound: <=4 boxes per <